Q)REST
Consider we need to introduce a REST api GET call, which will fetch around 1 lakh record from DB and send as a 
response to user. How we can achieve this in real time.

1. First, clarify the requirement
Before implementing the API, I would ask a few questions:
- Does the client really need all 100,000 records in a single response, or can it fetch them in pages?
- How large is each record? Are we returning 5 fields or 50 fields?
- Is this an interactive UI request, a reporting use case, or a bulk export?
- What are the expected response-time SLA and concurrent-user load?
- Does the client need a consistent snapshot of the data while paging?
These questions matter because 100,000 small records and 100,000 large records have very different performance characteristics.
My default recommendation would be pagination for interactive APIs and asynchronous export for bulk downloads.
2. Recommended production architecture
Client / UI
Requests a page or starts an export

Spring Boot REST API
Authentication, validation, rate limits

Paginated query
Small, bounded result sets for UI consumption


Export job
Chunked processing for large downloads



Database / Export storage
Indexed queries, bounded memory, controlled concurrency


I would choose the implementation based on the actual use case rather than assuming that returning all records in one HTTP response is always the best design.
3. Approach 1 — Pagination (my first choice for a REST API)
Instead of fetching 100,000 records at once, return a limited number of records per request.
For example:
GET /api/employees?page=0&size=100
GET /api/employees?page=1&size=100
GET /api/employees?page=2&size=100


The client fetches only the page it needs. This reduces the amount of data transferred per request and bounds application memory for each response.
Spring Boot implementation
Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Employee, Long> {

    Page<Employee> findAll(Pageable pageable);
}


Service
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    private final EmployeeRepository repository;

    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public Page<EmployeeResponse> getEmployees(Pageable pageable) {
        return repository.findAll(pageable)
                .map(employee -> new EmployeeResponse(
                        employee.getId(),
                        employee.getName(),
                        employee.getDepartment()
                ));
    }
}


Assume EmployeeResponse is a record containing the three fields shown above.
Controller
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/employees")
public class EmployeeController {

    private final EmployeeService employeeService;

    public EmployeeController(EmployeeService employeeService) {
        this.employeeService = employeeService;
    }

    @GetMapping
    public Page<EmployeeResponse> getEmployees(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size) {

        if (page < 0 || size < 1 || size > 500) {
            throw new ResponseStatusException(
                    HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST,
                    "Invalid page or page size"
            );
        }

        Pageable pageable = PageRequest.of(
                page,
                size,
                Sort.by(Sort.Direction.ASC, "id")
        );

        return employeeService.getEmployees(pageable);
    }
}


Imports such as Page, Pageable, PageRequest, Sort, HttpStatus, and ResponseStatusException are omitted for brevity.
What does the response look like?
For GET /api/employees?page=0&size=100, Spring Data returns a page containing employee data and pagination metadata, typically including the total number of elements and pages.
The client can use that metadata to decide whether to fetch the next page.
What would I improve in production?
- Apply a maximum page size; never allow an unrestricted client to request millions of rows.
- Return a DTO or database projection instead of serializing full JPA entities.
- Fetch only the columns the client actually needs.
- Add suitable database indexes for the filtering and sorting criteria.
- Avoid N+1 queries when loading related entities.
- Consider whether the exact total count is needed; Page can require an additional count query.
- Validate sorting fields rather than allowing arbitrary client-provided sort expressions.
Interview point: Pagination alone does not guarantee good performance. A query that scans an unindexed table or performs expensive joins can still be slow, even if it returns only 100 records.

4. Offset pagination vs keyset pagination
This is a useful distinction to explain at senior level.
Offset pagination
SELECT id, name, department
FROM employee
ORDER BY id
OFFSET 50000 ROWS
FETCH NEXT 100 ROWS ONLY;


This is conceptually similar to requesting a later page using Spring Data's PageRequest.
The drawback is that the database may still need to scan or skip many rows to reach a large offset. Performance can deteriorate on deep pages, depending on the database and query plan.
Keyset pagination (seek pagination)
Instead of skipping 50,000 rows, use the last employee ID returned by the previous request.
SELECT id, name, department
FROM employee
WHERE id > :lastSeenId
ORDER BY id
FETCH FIRST 100 ROWS ONLY;


The client could call:
GET /api/employees?limit=100&afterId=50000


The next response contains the records after ID 50000, and the last returned ID becomes the next cursor.
Why I prefer keyset for deep pagination
It avoids repeatedly skipping large numbers of rows and works well with an appropriate index on the cursor columns.


What to watch out for
The cursor should use a stable, deterministic ordering. If the sort field is not unique, use a unique tie-breaker such as (created_at, id). Concurrent inserts and updates can still affect which records are observed unless a consistent snapshot or equivalent strategy is used.



For 100,000 records, I would consider keyset pagination when clients need to traverse a large dataset, particularly if deep offset queries become expensive. Offset pagination remains perfectly reasonable for ordinary UI pages.

5. Approach 2 — If the user really needs all 100,000 records
Suppose the requirement is a report download, data migration, or bulk integration. The consumer genuinely needs every record.
In that case, I would generally prefer an asynchronous export job over making one large synchronous GET request.
A typical design is:
1. Client calls POST /api/employee-exports to initiate the export.
2. Server returns 202 Accepted with an export job ID.
3. A background worker reads records in chunks or streams them from the database cursor.
4. The worker writes the result to CSV, JSON Lines, or another suitable file format.
5. The client checks the job status and downloads the completed file.
Example:
POST /api/employee-exports


Response:
HTTP/1.1 202 Accepted
Content-Type: application/json


{
  "jobId": "exp-12345",
  "status": "ACCEPTED"
}


The client can later call:
GET /api/employee-exports/exp-12345


Once complete, the status endpoint can provide a download link.
Why is this better for bulk data?
- It avoids keeping a long-running HTTP request open while the database processes all records.
- It can handle transient failures and retries more deliberately.
- It lets you limit the number of simultaneous exports.
- It supports progress tracking, cancellation, and download expiration.
- It allows large results to be written incrementally without retaining the entire dataset in heap memory.
In a real production system, I would secure the generated file, enforce retention and expiration policies, and authorize downloads per user or tenant.

6. Approach 3 — Streaming the response directly
What if the interviewer says:
“The requirement is fixed. The API must return all 100,000 records in one HTTP response. You cannot introduce pagination or an export job. How would you implement it?”

Then I would consider streaming, provided the expected response size, latency, and client behavior make it practical.
Instead of building a Java List containing all 100,000 records and then serializing the entire list, the server writes records to the response incrementally.
Spring MVC provides StreamingResponseBody for this purpose.
@GetMapping(
    value = "/export",
    produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE
)
public ResponseEntity<StreamingResponseBody> exportEmployees() {

    StreamingResponseBody stream = outputStream -> {
        try (JsonGenerator json =
                     new JsonFactory().createGenerator(outputStream)) {

            json.writeStartArray();

            employeeService.writeEmployees(json);

            json.writeEndArray();
        }
    };

    return ResponseEntity.ok(stream);
}


The service would write each DTO as it is retrieved:
public void writeEmployees(JsonGenerator json)
        throws IOException {

    try (Stream<EmployeeProjection> employees =
                 repository.streamAllEmployees()) {

        employees.forEach(employee -> {
            try {
                json.writeStartObject();
                json.writeNumberField("id", employee.getId());
                json.writeStringField("name", employee.getName());
                json.writeStringField(
                        "department", employee.getDepartment());
                json.writeEndObject();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                throw new UncheckedIOException(e);
            }
        });
    }
}


Here, JsonGenerator is from Jackson, and EmployeeProjection represents an interface projection or equivalent DTO-oriented database result.
This is illustrative production-style pseudocode: the repository streaming method must be implemented for the actual database and persistence provider.
Important streaming considerations
1. Database cursor and fetch size: Configure and verify database-specific cursor/fetch behavior. A Java Stream alone does not guarantee that the driver avoids buffering all rows.
2. Transaction and resource lifecycle: Keep the required transaction and database resources open while reading, and close them reliably.
3. Connection pool exhaustion: A slow client can keep a database connection occupied for a long time.
4. Servlet thread capacity: StreamingResponseBody does not automatically make the whole application non-blocking. Configure and monitor asynchronous request execution.
5. Partial responses: If the database fails after output begins, the server may be unable to return a clean JSON error response because part of the response has already been sent.
6. Response size and compression: Measure the actual payload, network time, and memory consumption under concurrent load.
For these reasons, streaming is useful when a single response is mandatory, but I would not choose it automatically for every large GET endpoint.

7. How I would optimize the database and JVM
At your experience level, explain that performance must be measured across the entire request path, not just the controller.
Area	My approach
SQL	Select only required columns and inspect the execution plan
Indexing	Index appropriate filter and ordering columns
ORM	Use projections; avoid N+1 queries and unnecessary entity hydration
Memory	Avoid accumulating 100,000 entities and DTOs in a list
Database access	Use bounded pages, JDBC fetch sizes, or verified cursor streaming
HTTP	Measure payload size, serialization time, compression, and transfer time
Concurrency	Apply connection-pool limits, request limits, and backpressure where appropriate
Observability	Track query latency, API p95/p99 latency, heap usage, GC, timeouts, and error rates
I would also run a load test with realistic record sizes and expected concurrent users. An endpoint that works for one user may fail when 50 users request the same 100,000 records simultaneously.
A rough example: if the serialized response averages 500 bytes per record, 100,000 records represent about 50 MB before considering response compression and protocol overhead. The actual size can be much larger if records contain nested objects or long text fields.

8. What about Java virtual threads or WebFlux?
These can be useful, but neither is the first solution to this problem.
- Virtual threads: Can reduce the cost of blocking Java threads in suitable applications. They do not eliminate database limits, heap usage, network bandwidth constraints, or slow-client problems.
- Spring WebFlux: Can help with highly concurrent I/O-bound workloads when the entire relevant stack supports non-blocking operations. It does not automatically make a blocking JPA query non-blocking.
- Reactive database access: Can provide non-blocking database I/O when supported by the chosen driver and database stack, but it still needs appropriate query design and backpressure.
My first priorities would be query efficiency, bounded memory, a suitable response strategy, and concurrency control.


